+# ADR 0002: Directional flow-control gates
+
+- Status: Proposed
+- Date: 2026-10-08
+- Related: #742, #735
+
+## Context
+
+The flow controller owns independent send and receive byte windows but serializes
+both under one connection-wide gate. Opposite-direction operations therefore
+compete for that gate even when they share no mutable credit arithmetic. Removing
+that coupling is a bounded architecture experiment; it is not evidence that a
+particular RPC throughput, CPU or allocation regression has been resolved.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep the existing single send map and single receive map, with their current
+leases, pooling limits, credit windows, pending-capacity bound and admission rules.
+Assign each direction its own `System.Threading.Lock`:
+
+- Send owns its states and pooled list, generation counter, connection credit,
+  active and pending-capacity counts, and all waiter admission/cancellation fields.
+  First-admission lease capture belongs to this same domain.
+- Receive owns its states and pooled list, generation counter, connection credit,
+  pending-consumed aggregate and queued cross-stream updates.
+- Window and limit configuration is immutable and shared. The existing volatile
+  empty-queue probe is advisory; queue access remains under the receive gate.
+- The terminal exception is the only mutable shared lifecycle field. `Complete`
+  acquires send then receive, and only then publishes the first exception and
+  clears both domains. Readers hold either domain, so they observe the transition
+  before or after publication. No other controller path nests the gates.
+- Waiter completions, cancellation registration and continuations stay outside
+  controller-owned gate scopes. Admission already committed before terminal
+  publication can still complete successfully, as in the single-gate version.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Independent directions can make progress concurrently. Same-direction operations
+still serialize. Termination takes two gates and may hold send while waiting for
+an in-flight receive transition. Future changes must not acquire send while
+holding receive, or perform cross-direction arithmetic under only one gate.
+
+Every controller allocates one additional fixed-size `Lock` and stores one extra
+reference. Runtime object layout determines the exact per-connection byte cost.
+This is an intentional fixed-memory increase; it is not a zero-allocation-change
+claim for connection creation. No per-item allocation saving is inferred from the
+source change. Contended runtime lock machinery can also allocate independently
+of the application-level credit state.
+
+The wire protocol and public API do not change. Tests and opt-in diagnostics that
+reflect the private send gate must use `_sendGate` and retain its `Lock` type;
+locking its boxed/object-typed value would exercise a different monitor.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- Retain the one gate: simplest terminal coordination, but keeps unrelated
+  directional operations serialized.
+- Per-stream gates or a second lookup map: substantially broader ownership and
+  ordering changes than this experiment requires.
+- Change batching, windows or fairness: changes the comparison and existing
+  backpressure contract instead of isolating directional serialization.
+
+## Validation
+
+`StreamFlowControllerDirectionalGateTests` forces cross-thread progress while the
+other direction is held, send/receive pooled-state reuse, first-admission capacity
+and cancellation, and a terminal transition blocked between its two acquisitions.
+The original first-admission, stale-lease, waiter, allocation and transport tests
+remain required. The cross-direction progress cases are portable negative controls
+against the unchanged single-gate source.
+
+The original full dev-versus-final performance acceptance, CPU and allocation
+checks, NativeAOT evidence and exact final-source CI remain necessary. A small
+pure-controller mixed-direction diagnostic establishes mechanism only. Follow the
+canonical [Fast checks](../pr-fast.md) and
+[maintainability policy](../../eng/maintainability.md); do not relax their budgets.
